 \subsection{An End-to-End Request Example}
 A changing observation precedes a stable document. CALC moves eligible document content forward; the first raw business reply supplies a query witness. A successful check commits eligible stable blocks for later authorized requests. A new query needs its own decision but may share the prefix identity. Rejection retains the raw reply and promotes no candidate state.
 
+\subsection{Registration and the Trusted Service Boundary}
+\label{sec:registration}
+The operator registers segment identities and bytes, dependencies, template version, authorization domain, and the eligible publication boundary. Stability and visibility are distinct declarations: content can remain unchanged while still being tenant-private. Tenants cannot grant themselves shared-state registration authority.
+
+Three checks serve different purposes. The CPU registration helper checks issuer/role, authentication tag, content hash, identity uniqueness, version/domain presence, and whether publication contradicts declared privacy or mutability. It is an audit helper, not an integrated public registration endpoint. The evaluated deployment client checks the HMAC binding before selecting compiled execution; failure selects raw. In-engine PSTP independently controls candidate-prefix publication. Thus declaration consistency, request binding, and transactional visibility have separate enforcement points.
+
+The application, controller, runtime, and cache manager are trusted. The application's authenticated user-to-domain mapping must mediate engine access: the evaluated domain binding has a wildcard tenant field and does not authenticate arbitrary tenant labels. A valid hash/tag establishes byte identity and binding authenticity, not confidentiality. A trusted operator's consistent but incorrect private-to-shared declaration remains outside these checks (Section~\ref{sec:trust}).
+
 \subsection{Contract-Aware Layout Compiler}
 \label{sec:calc}
 CALC operates on the registered dependency graph. Stable segments may be split into contiguous, character-preserving units to construct a bounded layout ladder. A stable-priority topological traversal moves only eligible units, respects dependency edges, and breaks ties by original order. For the 16-unit ladder, L1, L2, and L3 expose 4, 12, and 16 eligible units, respectively; L0 retains raw order. Full retokenization determines the actual stable-prefix boundary of each rendered candidate.
